fish_species_catalog.gd:

```gdscript
class_name FishSpeciesCatalog
extends RefCounted
# CFG-001: Pure-data fish species catalog validator... (comments)

const ERR_FILE_MISSING := "FILE_MISSING"
const ERR_FILE_OPEN := "FILE_OPEN"
const ERR_JSON_PARSE := "JSON_PARSE"
const ERR_BAD_VERSION := "BAD_VERSION"
const ERR_BAD_SHAPE := "BAD_SHAPE"
const ERR_EMPTY_SPECIES := "EMPTY_SPECIES"
const ERR_BAD_ID_PATTERN := "BAD_ID_PATTERN"
const ERR_DUP_ID := "DUP_ID"
const ERR_BAD_RARITY := "BAD_RARITY"
const ERR_BAD_WEIGHT := "BAD_WEIGHT"
const ERR_BAD_PRICE := "BAD_PRICE"
const ERR_BAD_ASSET := "BAD_ASSET"

const CATALOG_VERSION := 1
const RARITIES := ["common", "uncommon", "rare"]
const SPECIES_FIELDS := ["id", "name_zh", "rarity", "catch_weight", "base_price", "asset_key"]
const ID_PATTERN := "^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$"

static func load_catalog(path: String) -> Dictionary:
	if not FileAccess.file_exists(path):
		return _fail(ERR_FILE_MISSING)
	var file := FileAccess.open(path, FileAccess.READ)
	if file == null:
		return _fail(ERR_FILE_OPEN)
	var text: String = file.get_as_text()
	file = null
	if text.is_empty():
		return _fail(ERR_JSON_PARSE)
	var json := JSON.new()
	if json.parse(text) != OK:
		return _fail(ERR_JSON_PARSE)
	var data: Variant = json.data
	if not (data is Dictionary):
		return _fail(ERR_JSON_PARSE)
	var root: Dictionary = data
	if not _has_exact_keys(root, ["catalog_version", "species"]):
		return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)
	if not _is_int_equal(root["catalog_version"], CATALOG_VERSION):
		return _fail(ERR_BAD_VERSION)
	var raw_species: Variant = root["species"]
	if not (raw_species is Array):
		return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)
	var arr: Array = raw_species
	if arr.is_empty():
		return _fail(ERR_EMPTY_SPECIES)
	var id_re := RegEx.new()
	if id_re.compile(ID_PATTERN) != OK:
		return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)  # shouldn't happen
	var seen := {}
	for element in arr:
		if not (element is Dictionary):
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)
		var sp: Dictionary = element
		if not _has_exact_keys(sp, SPECIES_FIELDS):
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)
		var id_value: Variant = sp["id"]
		if not (id_value is String) or (id_value as String).is_empty() or id_re.search(id_value) == null:
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_ID_PATTERN)
		var name_value: Variant = sp["name_zh"]
		if not (name_value is String) or (name_value as String).is_empty():
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_SHAPE)
		if not (sp["rarity"] in RARITIES):
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_RARITY)
		if not _is_positive_number(sp["catch_weight"]):
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_WEIGHT)
		if not _is_nonneg_int(sp["base_price"]):
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_PRICE)
		var asset: Variant = sp["asset_key"]
		if not (asset is String) or (asset as String).is_empty():
			return _fail(ERR_BAD_ASSET)
		var sid: String = id_value
		if seen.has(sid):
			return _fail(ERR_DUP_ID)
		seen[sid] = true
	return {"ok": true, "error_code": "", "species": arr.duplicate(true)}
```

Wait — the plan says dedup comes after per-element field checks (two passes); my single pass interleaves dedup into the element loop. In dup_id.json, both elements are otherwise valid so the result is the same. Plan's order: per-element all field checks, then pairwise compare. My per-element approach is subtly different (duplicate on element #1 vs weight error on element #2 → my version reports DUP_ID first... e.g., species [a, b] where b duplicates a but b has bad weight — plan would report BAD_WEIGHT first (field pass before dedup pass)). No such case in the fixtures. For fidelity to plan, do 2 passes: first loop validates all fields; second loop checks duplicates. Easy enough.

Also, plan's `search`: `id_re.search(str) != null` — but a full anchor is needed; RegEx's `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$` search: Godot's RegEx uses PCRE, `$` matches before a trailing newline; id "river_carp\n" would pass. Use `\A...\z`? Godot 4's RegEx supports those? PCRE supports \A and \z; but keep it simple. Or compare: `m.get_string(0) == id_value` — full coverage: `var m = id_re.search(v); if m == null or m.get_string(0) != v: fail`. This is robust against the $-newline quirk. Do that.

Helper `_is_int_equal(v, expected)`: `(v is int and v == expected) or (v is float and is_equal_approx(v, expected))`. For version compare use exact: `v is int and int(v)==1` or `v is float and fract==0 and int(v)==1`. Simpler: `_is_nonneg_int(v) and v == expected`... let me write:

```
static func _is_integral(v: Variant) -> bool:
	if v is int: return true
	if v is float: return is_finite(v) and fract(absf(v)) == 0.0
	return false
static func _as_int(v: Variant) -> int:
	return int(v)
```

_version==1_: `_is_integral(v) and int(v) == CATALOG_VERSION` — but negative version -1: _is_integral true, int(v)==1 false → BAD_VERSION. Good.

_is_positive_number(v): `(v is int and v>0) or (v is float and is_finite(v) and v>0.0)`.
_is_nonneg_int(v): `_is_integral(v) and v >= 0` → but v might be int... `float(v) >= 0.0`.

_has_exact_keys(d, keys): size equal and all present and no extras:
```
if d.size() != keys.size(): return false
for k in keys: if not d.has(k): return false
return true
```
Size-equal + all-present ⇒ no extras. Good.

_fail(code): `return {"ok": false, "error_code": code, "species": []}`.

